we have a major update this morning  about our ongoing CBS News investigation  into reports of carbon monoxide seeping  into Ford Explorers the automaker is now  offering free repairs 1.3 million owners  of the popular SUV will begin receiving  notices today they affect models from  2011 to 2017 but the automaker is  stopping short of recalling the Explorer  the watchdog group the center for auto  safety says anything short of a recall  is not enough CBS News has learned the  number of complaints is growing Kris Van  Cleave has been tracking this for the  last year and he is in Washington Chris  good morning good morning here is the  letter Ford sent out on Monday talking  about this piece of mind customer  satisfaction program the repairs involve  reprogramming the air conditioner  checking drainage valves along the back  of the vehicle and then checking all of  the seals here along the rear of the  vehicle this as the number of complaints  we have learned continues to grow  calling it quits  Steve Simmons unloads his tools from the  2015 Ford Explorer he bought used just a  couple of weeks earlier he made the  dealer buy it back after he says he got  sick behind the wheel nausea headaches  dizziness blurred vision after taking  blood tests Simmons doctor diagnosed him  with carbon monoxide poisoning what  would have happened to me and my wife we  would have been asleep off the road  doing at least 70 or 75 miles an hour  while we were doing it you really think  this could have killed you 100% not a  doubt in my mind the National Highway  Traffic Safety Administration is  investigating at least 1.3 million  2011 to 2017 Ford Explorers based on  reports of exhaust which contains carbon  monoxide  seeping into the passenger cabin nearly  1300 people have filed complaints with  the regulator and Ford acknowledged  getting more than 2,000 reports as of  August last year in the letter to  customers  Ford insists explorers are safe and its  investigation has not found carbon  monoxide levels that exceed what people  are exposed to everyday Nitsa says it's  found no actual evidence of carbon  monoxide poisoning that's just by  documented  cases like Louisiana police officer  brandy sickie who is now suing fort and  Simmons both were diagnosed after  driving an explorer Simmons just  launched Ford Explorer nightmare dot-com  hoping to rally support for a recall  what's it gonna take before decisive  action is taken now I know it's a lot of  money for Ford but I mean you know put a  value on human life Nitsa will not talk  about the timeline for its investigation  but did tell us it remains very  concerned about this potential safety  issue adding that the actions by Ford  here with this program they do not bring  closure to the Nitsa investigation the  agency is urging people who get this  letter to call their car dealer Gale  questions remain there thank you very  much Chris
 

